1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,488
A bus brakes hard, and
everyone lurches forward.

2
00:00:03,488 --> 00:00:06,047
It starts suddenly, and they fall back.

3
00:00:06,047 --> 00:00:08,766
A puck glides on and on across smooth ice.

4
00:00:09,166 --> 00:00:10,627
What links all three?

5
00:00:10,627 --> 00:00:12,525
One law, written by Newton.

6
00:00:12,975 --> 00:00:16,421
Aarav rides home with a water
bottle in his front basket.

7
00:00:16,421 --> 00:00:18,777
At the college gate, he brakes hard.

8
00:00:19,127 --> 00:00:20,376
The bicycle stops.

9
00:00:20,376 --> 00:00:23,467
But the bottle flies
forward, out of the basket.

10
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:25,148
Nothing pushed it forward.

11
00:00:25,148 --> 00:00:26,558
So why did it move?

12
00:00:27,058 --> 00:00:29,409
Let's find out, with Newton's first law.

13
00:00:30,009 --> 00:00:31,436
First, the law itself.

14
00:00:31,786 --> 00:00:36,142
Isaac Newton published it in sixteen
eighty seven, building on Galileo's

15
00:00:36,142 --> 00:00:37,042
work.

16
00:00:37,102 --> 00:00:41,704
Every body stays at rest, or keeps
moving at a constant speed in a straight

17
00:00:41,704 --> 00:00:42,604
line,

18
00:00:42,545 --> 00:00:45,289
unless an external force makes it change.

19
00:00:45,289 --> 00:00:48,608
In short: no net force,
no change in velocity.

20
00:00:49,258 --> 00:00:51,655
Let's test it on the motion notebook.

21
00:00:51,655 --> 00:00:55,353
A two kilogram block slides
off at four metres per second.

22
00:00:55,703 --> 00:00:58,195
On a rough floor, friction is strong.

23
00:00:58,195 --> 00:00:59,803
The block stops quickly.

24
00:00:59,803 --> 00:01:01,848
Its velocity line drops steeply.

25
00:01:02,248 --> 00:01:03,771
Make the floor smoother.

26
00:01:03,771 --> 00:01:06,548
Less friction, so the
line drops more gently.

27
00:01:06,948 --> 00:01:08,092
Smoother still.

28
00:01:08,092 --> 00:01:09,451
It slides farther.

29
00:01:11,051 --> 00:01:13,913
Now turn friction all
the way down to zero.

30
00:01:14,313 --> 00:01:15,640
The line goes flat.

31
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:19,917
Four metres per second, and it
stays four, for as long as we watch.

32
00:01:20,567 --> 00:01:21,721
Slow-mo replay.

33
00:01:21,721 --> 00:01:23,984
Freeze, and draw every force.

34
00:01:24,334 --> 00:01:25,700
Weight pulls down.

35
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:28,082
The floor pushes up just as hard.

36
00:01:28,082 --> 00:01:29,079
They cancel.

37
00:01:29,479 --> 00:01:32,117
With no friction, nothing is left over.

38
00:01:32,117 --> 00:01:35,608
The net force is zero, so
the velocity never changes.

39
00:01:36,258 --> 00:01:38,427
Now the law in three small steps.

40
00:01:38,777 --> 00:01:39,677
Step one.

41
00:01:39,604 --> 00:01:41,269
Add up all the forces.

42
00:01:41,269 --> 00:01:43,760
The net force, sigma F, is zero.

43
00:01:44,510 --> 00:01:45,474
Step two.

44
00:01:45,474 --> 00:01:48,485
Zero net force means zero acceleration.

45
00:01:49,235 --> 00:01:50,191
Step three.

46
00:01:50,191 --> 00:01:52,216
So the velocity is constant.

47
00:01:52,216 --> 00:01:54,575
If it was zero, it stays zero.

48
00:01:54,575 --> 00:01:56,663
Rest is just a special case.

49
00:01:57,413 --> 00:01:58,580
Remember this.

50
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:02,606
Zero net force means constant
velocity, not always rest.

51
00:02:03,306 --> 00:02:04,487
Now, the bus.

52
00:02:04,487 --> 00:02:05,568
Link by link.

53
00:02:05,918 --> 00:02:07,009
The bus brakes.

54
00:02:07,009 --> 00:02:10,208
The brakes act on the bus,
so the bus slows down.

55
00:02:10,608 --> 00:02:13,911
Friction from the floor slows
your feet along with the bus.

56
00:02:14,311 --> 00:02:16,384
But nothing slows your upper body.

57
00:02:16,384 --> 00:02:19,991
By the first law, it keeps
moving forward at the old speed.

58
00:02:20,391 --> 00:02:22,549
Feet slow down, body does not.

59
00:02:22,549 --> 00:02:24,053
So you lurch forward.

60
00:02:24,703 --> 00:02:27,533
When the bus starts, it
is the other way round.

61
00:02:27,533 --> 00:02:31,906
Your feet move off with the bus, your
body stays behind, and you fall back.

62
00:02:32,506 --> 00:02:33,582
Now you try.

63
00:02:33,582 --> 00:02:35,849
A bus takes a sharp left turn.

64
00:02:35,849 --> 00:02:38,471
Which way do standing passengers lean?

65
00:02:38,471 --> 00:02:39,808
Pause and decide.

66
00:02:43,008 --> 00:02:43,933
To the right!

67
00:02:43,933 --> 00:02:47,101
The bus turns left, but your
body keeps going straight.

68
00:02:47,101 --> 00:02:49,524
So you lean outward, away from the turn.

69
00:02:50,174 --> 00:02:51,284
Side by side.

70
00:02:51,284 --> 00:02:53,247
A book resting on a table.

71
00:02:53,247 --> 00:02:55,889
Its weight and the table's push cancel.

72
00:02:55,889 --> 00:02:58,315
Velocity zero, and it stays zero.

73
00:02:58,715 --> 00:03:02,155
A car cruising at a steady
speed on a straight road.

74
00:03:02,155 --> 00:03:04,625
The forward push and the drag cancel.

75
00:03:04,625 --> 00:03:05,870
Velocity constant.

76
00:03:06,470 --> 00:03:07,370
Your turn.

77
00:03:07,339 --> 00:03:10,786
The car moves at a steady
sixty kilometres per hour.

78
00:03:10,786 --> 00:03:12,855
What is the net force on it?

79
00:03:12,855 --> 00:03:14,134
Pause and decide.

80
00:03:17,334 --> 00:03:18,234
Zero!

81
00:03:17,932 --> 00:03:21,660
Steady speed in a straight
line means no acceleration.

82
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:25,495
The forward push exactly
balances friction and air drag.

83
00:03:26,145 --> 00:03:27,880
Now, one worked example.

84
00:03:28,230 --> 00:03:32,970
A twelve hundred kilogram car cruises
at a steady twenty metres per second.

85
00:03:32,970 --> 00:03:36,481
Friction and air drag together
are six hundred newtons.

86
00:03:36,481 --> 00:03:39,386
Find the driving force
and the normal force.

87
00:03:39,886 --> 00:03:44,360
Constant velocity, so the net force
is zero, both along the road and up

88
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:45,260
and down.

89
00:03:45,901 --> 00:03:49,166
Along the road, the driving
force equals the drag.

90
00:03:49,166 --> 00:03:51,132
Six hundred newtons, forward.

91
00:03:51,882 --> 00:03:55,037
Up and down, the normal
force equals the weight.

92
00:03:55,037 --> 00:03:57,445
Twelve hundred times nine point eight.

93
00:03:57,445 --> 00:04:00,888
That is eleven thousand seven
hundred and sixty newtons.

94
00:04:01,538 --> 00:04:03,004
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:03,354 --> 00:04:07,798
Many students say: if the net force
is zero, the body must be at rest.

96
00:04:08,198 --> 00:04:10,981
But zero net force
means zero acceleration.

97
00:04:10,981 --> 00:04:15,866
The body can be at rest, or moving at
a constant velocity, like the cruising

98
00:04:15,866 --> 00:04:16,766
car.

99
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:18,452
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:18,452 --> 00:04:22,710
In concept questions on net force,
and in finding a missing force when

101
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:24,238
a body moves steadily.

102
00:04:24,838 --> 00:04:26,143
Exam level now.

103
00:04:26,143 --> 00:04:31,165
A two kilogram particle moves at a
constant velocity of three i plus four

104
00:04:31,165 --> 00:04:32,813
j metres per second.

105
00:04:32,813 --> 00:04:38,122
Two forces act: two i minus three
j, and minus five i plus j newtons.

106
00:04:38,122 --> 00:04:40,058
What is the third force?

107
00:04:40,058 --> 00:04:41,419
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:46,819 --> 00:04:48,264
The answer is B.

109
00:04:48,264 --> 00:04:50,460
Three i plus two j newtons.

110
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:54,192
Constant velocity means
the net force is zero.

111
00:04:54,692 --> 00:04:56,524
Add the two known forces.

112
00:04:56,524 --> 00:04:58,672
Two minus five is minus three.

113
00:04:58,672 --> 00:05:01,084
Minus three plus one is minus two.

114
00:05:01,584 --> 00:05:03,533
The third force cancels them.

115
00:05:03,533 --> 00:05:05,443
Plus three i, plus two j.

116
00:05:05,443 --> 00:05:08,201
The mass and the velocity were not needed.

117
00:05:08,801 --> 00:05:10,260
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:11,759
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:14,959 --> 00:05:16,890
Can you answer these without help?

120
00:05:17,290 --> 00:05:18,190
One.

121
00:05:17,799 --> 00:05:21,766
Why does the bottle fly out of
the basket when Aarav brakes?

122
00:05:22,666 --> 00:05:23,566
Two.

123
00:05:23,135 --> 00:05:26,230
A car moves at a steady
speed in a straight line.

124
00:05:26,230 --> 00:05:28,136
What is the net force on it?

125
00:05:29,036 --> 00:05:29,936
Three.

126
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:32,539
Why do you fall back when
a bus starts suddenly?

127
00:05:33,639 --> 00:05:38,480
Next time: if zero net force means
no change, how much change does a net

128
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:39,418
force make?

129
00:05:39,418 --> 00:05:41,833
We find out in Newton's second law.
